{
  "$comment": [
    "TLD risk data for the `suspicious-tld` check (PLAN.md §2).",
    "",
    "`risky` is graded, not binary. A `.zip` link is not proof of anything — it is one weak signal",
    "that only matters when it stacks with others. Weighting them equally would make the check a",
    "false-positive machine on the many legitimate sites using cheap TLDs.",
    "",
    "  tier 3 — the TLD is itself a deception vector (.zip/.mov collide with filenames) or has",
    "           near-total abuse rates in published registry data.",
    "  tier 2 — free or near-free registration, historically dominated by abuse.",
    "  tier 1 — cheap and mixed-use. Plenty of legitimate sites live here.",
    "",
    "`safe` TLDs are gated or high-trust registries. They do not subtract score; they suppress the",
    "`suspicious-tld` signal so it can never fire on a bank or a university.",
    "",
    "Multi-part public suffixes go in `publicSuffixes` — parse.js needs them to compute eTLD+1",
    "correctly, or 'example.co.uk' reads as registrable domain 'co.uk' and every check misfires."
  ],
